WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.559
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.327
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:16.923
Unit twenty-four: Global Education Goals. Today you will strengthen comparisons with far, much and by far.

4
00:00:17.223 --> 00:00:19.771
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.036
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:26.128
Education.

7
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:30.358
Education means the process of learning knowledge and skills.

8
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.542
Education opens doors to better jobs.

9
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.675
Your turn. Say: Education.

10
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:40.639
Education.

11
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:42.663
SDG 4.

12
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.297
SDG 4 means the United Nations goal for inclusive and quality education.

13
00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:52.613
SDG 4 promises learning for all by 2030.

14
00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:55.881
Your turn. Say: SDG 4.

15
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:00.605
SDG 4.

16
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.293
Access.

17
00:01:03.283 --> 00:01:06.409
Access means the ability to reach or use something.

18
00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:10.003
Access to school is limited in remote villages.

19
00:01:10.617 --> 00:01:13.466
Your turn. Say: Access.

20
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.382
Access.

21
00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:19.025
Quality.

22
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:22.864
Quality means how good or effective something is.

23
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.705
Quality matters as much as quantity.

24
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:29.004
Your turn. Say: Quality.

25
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:32.705
Quality.

26
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.544
Literacy.

27
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.176
Literacy means the ability to read and write.

28
00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:41.096
Literacy rose after the new programme.

29
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:44.231
Your turn. Say: Literacy.

30
00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:48.056
Literacy.

31
00:01:48.817 --> 00:01:50.305
Girls' education.

32
00:01:51.317 --> 00:01:55.325
Girls' education means schooling opportunities for girls and young women.

33
00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:58.601
Girls' education improves family health.

34
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:02.415
Your turn. Say: Girls' education.

35
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:06.674
Girls' education.

36
00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:08.383
Teachers.

37
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:12.846
Teachers means the people whose job is to educate students.

38
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.740
Teachers shape every child's future.

39
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.188
Your turn. Say: Teachers.

40
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:23.139
Teachers.

41
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:24.652
Funding.

42
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.752
Funding means money provided for a particular purpose.

43
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.222
Funding for schools was increased.

44
00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.190
Your turn. Say: Funding.

45
00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:37.942
Funding.

46
00:02:38.733 --> 00:02:42.105
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:49.236
Visal, a UN report says 'Funding for teachers is by far the most important factor.' What does 'by far' do?

48
00:02:50.350 --> 00:03:03.349
It strengthens a superlative: by far the most important. For comparatives we use far, much or considerably: 'Girls' education brings far greater benefits than expected.'

49
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.066
Can we weaken a comparison?

50
00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:20.509
Yes: slightly better, a little more access, not quite as high. 'Quality is nearly as important as access' shows two things almost equal. 'Twice as many' gives a precise ratio.

51
00:03:21.617 --> 00:03:23.477
Which mistakes should I avoid?

52
00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:36.482
Do not use 'very' before a comparative: not 'very better'. Say 'much better'. Avoid double comparatives such as 'more higher'. Use either -er or more, never both.

53
00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:39.238
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:44.149
Grammar focus: Modified comparatives and superlatives.

55
00:03:44.449 --> 00:03:45.683
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:46.383 --> 00:03:50.740
Funding for teachers is by far the most important factor in raising quality.

57
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.577
Girls' education brings far greater benefits than most people expect.

58
00:03:56.483 --> 00:04:00.503
Access is nearly as important as quality in SDG 4.

59
00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:09.494
Use much / far / considerably before comparatives (far greater) and by far before superlatives (by far the most). Never say very better or more higher.

60
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.994
Be careful! Don't say: This is very better than before.

61
00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:18.606
Say: This is much better than before.

62
00:04:18.906 --> 00:04:21.684
Use much, not very, before a comparative.

63
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.071
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:25.867 --> 00:04:27.321
Learning for Everyone.

65
00:04:27.621 --> 00:04:34.322
SDG 4 aims to give every child access to quality education, but progress has been far slower than hoped.

66
00:04:35.333 --> 00:04:43.324
Literacy rates are considerably higher in countries that invest in teachers, and girls' education brings by far the greatest long-term benefit.

67
00:04:44.333 --> 00:04:49.526
Funding matters, yet in many regions classrooms are still much more crowded than the standards recommend.

68
00:04:50.533 --> 00:04:56.016
Access is nearly as important as quality, because a school that children cannot reach teaches no one.

69
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.507
Where teachers are better trained and better supported, results improve far more quickly.

70
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.706
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.689
This is blank the best school in the region.

72
00:05:09.989 --> 00:05:13.666
A, very. B, by far. C, more.

73
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.130
The answer is B: by far.

74
00:05:20.283 --> 00:05:22.689
The new method is blank better than the old one.

75
00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:26.364
A, very. B, much. C, most.

76
00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.772
The answer is B: much.

77
00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:34.649
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:43.908
A, Girls' education is very more important.. B, Girls' education is far more important.. C, Girls' education is more important far..

79
00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:51.161
The answer is B: Girls' education is far more important..

80
00:05:52.317 --> 00:05:54.641
The ability to read and write is blank.

81
00:05:54.941 --> 00:05:58.351
A, literacy. B, funding. C, quality.

82
00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:03.760
The answer is A: literacy.

83
00:06:04.917 --> 00:06:08.530
According to the reading, what does a school that children cannot reach do?

84
00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:14.086
A, teaches no one. B, improves quality. C, reduces funding.

85
00:06:17.633 --> 00:06:19.737
The answer is A: teaches no one.

86
00:06:20.933 --> 00:06:24.422
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:25.283 --> 00:06:27.956
What would most improve education worldwide?

88
00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.285
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:38.585 --> 00:07:00.213
I believe investing in well-trained, well-paid teachers would improve education the most. Good teachers raise literacy and make schools worth attending. Funding must also reach girls and rural communities so that access is fair. Quality and access together matter far more than expensive buildings or technology alone.

90
00:07:00.513 --> 00:07:02.223
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:03.117 --> 00:07:08.381
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modified comparatives and superlatives.

92
00:07:08.681 --> 00:07:14.433
Today's value is educational equity. Every learner deserves a fair chance to succeed.

93
00:07:14.733 --> 00:07:17.432
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:17.732 --> 00:07:20.744
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

